---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('smsbump')!;
const path = '/apps/smsbump';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/smsbump');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/smsbump');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is SMSBump still a Shopify SMS option?",
    "answer": "No new rollout should depend on it. Yotpo scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025, and its current SMS service notice says the dashboard and background services are inactive. This page supports exit planning and historical requirements review."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does SMSBump include Yotpo Email?",
    "answer": "Both Email and SMS were covered by Yotpo’s sunset announcement. Pairing retired SMSBump with Yotpo Email is not a supported new lifecycle strategy. Evaluate a supported destination for each journey."
  },
  {
    "question": "Did Yotpo also retire Reviews and Loyalty?",
    "answer": "The messaging retirement does not establish that. Yotpo continues to present Reviews and Loyalty separately. Keep their contracts, credentials, data and active journeys in a distinct inventory; do not delete them as part of an SMS exit by assumption."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I export all SMSBump records now?",
    "answer": "Do not assume an inactive dashboard provides an export. Inventory files and evidence already held, ask the provider what remains available, and record gaps. Consent, suppressions, sender registrations and message history may require different recovery paths."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I keep my sending number?",
    "answer": "Confirm number ownership, type, destination support, registration and release or porting requirements with the relevant providers. A customer list transfer does not transfer a number or its approvals. Do not promise a cutover date until those dependencies are established."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare Postscript or another replacement?",
    "answer": "Use staff records to test phone normalization, permission, opt-out handling, replies, cart events and purchase suppression. Compare supported destination markets and the written usage quote. Historical SMSBump positioning is not a current capability comparison."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much does retired SMSBump cost?",
    "answer": "There is no current onboarding price to recommend. Reconcile legacy charges and obligations against your invoices and contract. Price the destination using platform, destination, segment, carrier and MMS charges plus migration labor."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can old recovery messages be replayed?",
    "answer": "Do not replay stale carts merely because data was imported. Reconcile pending queues and send history, deduplicate event IDs, and recheck current permission, purchases and suppression before any destination enrollment."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a safe rollback for this exit?",
    "answer": "Pause destination sends and forms if permission or event handling fails. Retain evidence and correct the mapping before resuming. A decommissioned SMSBump service is not a usable rollback sender."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">SMSBump is a historical Shopify SMS service, later associated with Yotpo SMS. Cart recovery, broadcasts, MMS and suite coordination explain why merchants used it; they do not make it available for a new rollout today.</p>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">Checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025</a>. The <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current SMS notice</a> confirms the dashboard and background services are inactive. Yotpo continues to present Reviews and Loyalty as separate products.</p>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">This review replaces legacy recommendations and unsubstantiated outcomes with an exit runbook. The exercises below are proposed acceptance fixtures, not production test results, recovery-rate benchmarks or automatic export promises.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">What the historical product solved</h2>
      <p>SMSBump’s historical appeal was a mobile messaging workflow alongside Shopify and the Yotpo ecosystem: cart reminders, promotional messages, visual MMS and subscriber capture. A shared vendor relationship could simplify procurement and operator navigation. That explains historical selection; it cannot justify adding recovery to an inactive dashboard.</p>
      <p>The useful requirement survives the service: a merchant may still need a cart reminder, a review request or a VIP announcement. Assign each journey to an actually supported sender. Preserve the distinction between a Review or Loyalty record and permission to send promotional SMS.</p>
      <p>MMS can show a product image and a reply channel can help a shopper ask a question. Neither guarantees more profitable recovery. Evaluate image rendering, reply routing and message cost in the destination with the catalog and markets you actually serve.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Inventory services before moving records</h2>
      <p>List the Shopify connection, legacy forms, embedded scripts, scheduled campaigns, recovery journeys, inbound numbers and external automations. Identify the owner, last known activity and evidence source for each. A decommission notice establishes inactivity of the service; it does not by itself settle every invoice or customer agreement.</p>
      <p>Keep continuing Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty integrations in a separate column. Record their API credentials and downstream consumers before revoking anything. A key used by Reviews should not be removed merely because an unrelated messaging connection is retired.</p>
      <p>Check the actual storefront for old form endpoints, consent copy and broken confirmation states. Replace or remove inactive collection paths. Submit a staff record through every remaining entry point and trace where it lands; a visible popup is not evidence that a subscriber record reaches a working sender.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Data recovery without an export guarantee</h2>
      <p>Inventory previously saved exports, invoices, consent archives, suppression files and message logs. Note file date, timezone, record count, field definitions and storage owner. Ask the provider which records remain accessible and through what process; the retired dashboard may not supply a new export.</p>
      <p>Build a destination mapping for normalized phone, country, Shopify customer ID, subscription state, consent timestamp and source, opt-out event and latest purchase. Preserve message status and journey identifiers where available. A missing consent record is a gap to resolve, not an invitation to infer permission from a past order.</p>
      <p>Keep a read-only copy of recovered files and transformation rules. Reconcile input and accepted counts, with separate rejection reasons for malformed numbers, duplicates and unresolved permission. Do not merge two people solely because a recycled phone number matches an old profile.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Number, registration and reply ownership</h2>
      <p>For every sender, establish whether it is a short code, toll-free number, long code or another supported identity, who owns it and which provider controls release. Ask the destination about country support, required approvals and porting sequence before setting a launch date.</p>
      <p>Phone portability, customer data portability and registration approval are separate dependencies. Record the provider’s confirmed steps and any unavailable information. Do not cancel a number-related agreement until the responsible providers establish what that does to a transfer in progress.</p>
      <p>Test inbound replies, STOP handling and customer-service routing on the supported destination. A successful outbound staff text does not prove replies reach an agent. Include an out-of-hours response path and a failed-delivery path. Preserve the current suppression state before enabling any campaign.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Four proposed replacement acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p><strong>Cart event:</strong> create a staff cart, abandon it, update its contents and then purchase before the planned send. Inspect cart ID, event ID, customer identity and purchase timestamp. Passing means a purchased cart is suppressed and an event retry does not create another reminder.</p>
      <p><strong>Review request:</strong> keep a continuing Reviews workflow intact while testing the supported message sender. Use fulfilled, unfulfilled and refunded staff orders. Establish which system owns scheduling; do not assume the former SMSBump timing service remains available.</p>
      <p><strong>VIP announcement:</strong> use one opted-in staff profile and one loyalty member without SMS permission. A tier label may select content, but must not grant channel eligibility. Change the tier before send and check whether the destination reevaluates the segment or uses a frozen audience.</p>
      <p><strong>Email and SMS collision:</strong> schedule both journeys on staff records, then trigger purchase, unsubscribe and an event retry. Define a priority rule with the existing email owner and inspect the queue after each change. Passing requires the documented suppression behavior, not a weekly manual export that misses intervening changes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Queue reconciliation at cutover</h2>
      <p>Inventory known pending campaigns and automations from saved schedules, connected systems and provider confirmation. Background-service inactivity does not prove that every external integration has stopped emitting events. Disable stale triggers and remove dead endpoints after recording dependencies.</p>
      <p>Choose a cutover timestamp and timezone. Record which sender owns each journey and which cohort is eligible. Use stable event IDs where supported to deduplicate retries; document a bounded deduplication rule when IDs are missing. Avoid re-enrolling an entire imported list into welcome or recovery by default.</p>
      <p>Recheck permission, suppressions and purchases at send time. Compare destination scheduled counts with the intended staff cohort before opening production eligibility. If prior send history cannot be recovered, use a conservative fresh-event boundary rather than pretending old journeys can be reconstructed exactly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">A skincare exit scenario, not a merchant result</h2>
      <p>Proposed planning case: a skincare store retains Yotpo Reviews and an existing email sender while replacing retired SMSBump. Its requirement is one supported mobile cart reminder and coordinated review requests. Its product images make an MMS test plausible, but do not establish a click or conversion advantage.</p>
      <p>First map Shopify customer and order IDs to the destination, then align phone format, permission and suppression. Leave working email recovery in place while staff fixtures run. The owner must demonstrate that a purchase prevents SMS and that review requests do not collide with a newly scheduled cart reminder.</p>
      <p>Compare Postscript as a specialized SMS candidate with a supported combined email/SMS destination. The former may preserve the existing email boundary; the latter changes ownership and migration work. A shared Yotpo account or familiar dashboard is not a selection criterion for an unavailable messaging service.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Economics for a supported destination</h2>
      <p>Request current rates by destination market and sender type, including platform minimums, carrier charges, SMS segments, MMS pricing and registration fees. Test long text, Unicode and links against actual billing rules. A nominal message count may differ from billed segments.</p>
      <p>Illustrative arithmetic only: assume 4,000 billed SMS segments at $0.02, 500 MMS at $0.05 and a $50 platform charge. Messaging costs $80 plus $25 plus $50, or $155 before other fees. These are invented planning inputs, not SMSBump or replacement quotes.</p>
      <p>If a controlled cohort adds 20 orders at $60 AOV and 50% contribution before messaging, that is $600. Subtract $155 messaging and an assumed $100 operating cost to leave $345 before incremental discounts, returns and other fees. Zero incremental orders would instead leave a $255 cost. Attributed revenue alone cannot establish the positive case.</p>
      <p>Keep offer value equal when comparing SMS with MMS and retain an eligible holdout. Report incremental contribution, opt-outs, replies requiring service and delivery failures. No universal 400-order threshold, 8% recovery rate or fixed monthly opt-out ceiling substitutes for the store’s baseline and permitted sending scope.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Staged launch and rollback</h2>
      <p>Complete recovery and number-dependency work first. Then validate staff imports with sends disabled. Inspect permission, phone formatting, source fields and rejection logs. Keep destination forms and campaign eligibility separate so one can be paused without deleting evidence.</p>
      <p>Launch only a limited fresh-event cohort after queue, purchase and opt-out fixtures pass. Assign one operator to approve journey ownership and one to reconcile counts. Set store-specific stop conditions for missing permission, duplicate touches, broken replies and unexpected charges.</p>
      <p>Rollback means pausing the supported destination, restoring a known working capture path if available and repairing the mapping. It cannot mean switching back to decommissioned SMSBump. Keep the email journey operating only within its own valid permission and suppression rules.</p>
      <p>After a stable cutover, remove obsolete scripts and revoke messaging-only credentials once dependencies are accounted for. Retain the transformation log, consent evidence and billing reconciliation according to the store’s applicable retention requirements. Do not delete continuing Reviews or Loyalty records as collateral cleanup.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Operational review after the exit</h2>
      <p>Reconcile fresh events, accepted profiles, queued messages, suppressions and delivered messages on the first sending days. Investigate differences using event IDs and timestamps. Opt-out updates must not wait for a weekly spreadsheet exchange if messages can leave in between.</p>
      <p>Review replies and service tickets alongside campaign performance. A creative that attracts clicks but generates unresolved sizing questions may add support costs. Check MMS rendering across staff devices and failed-media behavior before expanding imagery.</p>
      <p>Monthly, reconcile the destination invoice to billed segments and markets and confirm legacy obligations separately. Review the continuing Yotpo modules on their own usage and contracts. There is no substantiated savings figure or fixed recovery period for this exit.</p>
      <p>The final handoff names the supported sender for every journey, number owner, registration state, permission source, queue rule and pause control. An unresolved export or number dependency remains explicit in the runbook; dashboard familiarity does not close it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Read the alternatives as different decisions</h2>
      <p>The Postscript review is relevant to a dedicated SMS sender evaluation; Klaviyo-versus-Postscript frames combined channel ownership versus a separate SMS boundary. Re-run the fixtures in any candidate rather than importing historical feature rankings.</p>
      <p>The Yotpo Email and SMS review provides retirement context. It is not a route to new onboarding. Reviews and Loyalty can remain useful products while the messaging service is unavailable, so an exit decision should not be broadened into an unsupported claim that the entire company or suite ended.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card mb-12"><h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Compare the actual job</h2><ul>
      <li><a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript review</a> — specialized SMS evaluation</li>
      <li><a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> — combined versus separate channel ownership</li>
      <li><a class="link" href="/apps/yotpo-email-sms">Yotpo Email & SMS review</a> — retirement context</li>
      <li><a class="link" href="/apps/recart">Recart review</a> — another historical comparison to verify independently</li>
    </ul></div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="SMSBump Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
